{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And he led forth his trained men, etc.</b> Rashi explained that Eliezer alone was there, for three hundred and eighteen is the numerical value of the name אֱלִיעֶזֶר. But is it the way of Scripture to state a number that alludes to a single man? And what is the meaning of what he said, ״וְחֵלֶק הָאֲנָשִׁים אֲשֶׁר הָלְכוּ אִתִּי״ (Genesis 14:24) — when no one went with him but Eliezer alone? And what seems right to me to say regarding this is that even Rashi concedes that he took with him three hundred and eighteen actual men, for every person is obligated to act by way of nature to the full extent of what he finds in his power to do, and whatever nature lacks, the miracle will complete. But the difficulty remains: why did Abram take specifically the number three hundred and eighteen? And what does Scripture tell us by this? Rather, to teach that the battle was so overpowering against him, to the point that there was no possibility whatsoever by way of nature to defeat them — and despite everything Abraham went forth to battle, for he relied on the assurance that his Elohim would come to his aid. And it is a mnemonic for the matter: he took with him men numbering the equivalent of ״אֱלִיעֶזֶר״, to teach that Eliezer alone was there — for that victory was not accomplished through any human effort at all but only through divine aid alone. Therefore he pursued only as far as Dan, and there the Shekhinah withdrew on account of the calf that they were destined to set up in Dan; therefore his strength failed there, with the withdrawal of the divine aid. And this is what is stated, ״וַיֵּחָלֵק עֲלֵיהֶם לַיְלָה״ (Genesis 14:15) — that half the night remained for him to divide the night of Egypt, for then HaShem Himself went forth in the midst of Egypt. So too in this battle there was nothing but the aid of El Elyon, who delivered his adversaries into his hand." "Another matter: <b>that he took three hundred and eighteen men numbering the equivalent of Eliezer,</b> to teach that Eliezer was weighed against all of them, for the merit of Abram cleaved to him — for fine is the discourse of the servants of the patriarchs more than the Torah instruction of the sons, etc. And there are those who say ״וַיָּרֶק״ — that he emptied them of every weapon, and they went without weapons and relied on the salvation of HaShem, after the pattern of ״אָרִיק חַרְבִּי תּוֹרִישֵׁמוֹ יָדִי״ (Exodus 15:9), for Pharaoh said: even if I empty my sword and remove it entirely, nevertheless ״תּוֹרִישֵׁמוֹ יָדִי״ — my hand alone, without a sword."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "The word וַיָּרֶק can mean 'led forth' or 'emptied'; comment [1] exploits the second sense."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:18.543406911Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}